  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 616 PM EDT SUN AUG 2 2026\n\nThe models remain in good agreement with the long wave pattern \nthrough the end of next week. An upper level high pressure system \nwill remain centered across the western Atlantic, strengthening \nthrough the latter half of the work week. Further west, a \npersistent ridge centered near the Four Corners region will also \nintensify during the period. Meanwhile, a deeper upper level low \nspiraling over the vicinity of Hudson Bay will spread cyclonic \nflow over the north central U.S. through next week. By the start\nof next weekend, both ridges will have dampened somewhat, with \nmore model uncertainty regarding how much residual troughing can \nbe maintained across the Tennessee Valley and portions of the Deep\nSouth. In general, this will result in a continuation of \ndiurnally-driven convection each day through next week across \neastern Kentucky, with elevated humidity levels and near normal \ntemperatures expected for early August. \n\nThe models have trended more towards sustaining a closed low over \nthe Deep South on Tuesday, with higher PoPs favored across our\nterrain, and lesser chances north, further away from the forcing.\nThe upper low will drift closer to us on Wednesday, bringing\nhigher rain chances to eastern Kentucky. LREF mean PWATs continue\nto range in the 50-75th percentile range of climatology, but storm\nmotions look a bit slower this go around at around 10 kts, so\nlocally heavy rainfall will be possible. The upper level low will\ndampen across the region on Thursday, with some degree of \ntroughing remaining in place through the weekend, dependent on the\nstrength of upstream cyclonic flow and any potential short wave\nenergy migration. There still seems to be pretty good confidence \nin keeping any surface fronts north of the Ohio River, allowing \nfor only weak forcing across our area. As such, expect mainly \nafternoon/early evening PoPs in place each day through Sunday, but\nconvection should be more scattered in nature. Highs each day \nwill average in the lower to middle 80s. Lows will moderate \nslightly from the mid to upper 60s to near 70 by next weekend, \nwith elevated humidity levels in place.",
